  <body>
    <div id="root">
      <div data-edu style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
      <main>
        <h1>IXL | Math, Language Arts, Science, Social Studies, and Spanish</h1>
        <p>
          A free, open learning resource for students, teachers and anyone revising for an exam.
          Work through condensed topic notes, follow worked examples, then test yourself with
          practice questions and past-paper style problems. No account and no subscription.
        </p>

        <section>
          <h2>Academic subjects</h2>
          <p>
            Mathematics, Algebra, Geometry, Trigonometry, Calculus, Statistics and Probability,
            Biology, Cell Biology, Genetics, Ecology, Human Physiology, Chemistry, Atomic Structure,
            Chemical Bonding, Organic Chemistry, Physics, Mechanics, Electricity, Waves,
            Thermodynamics, History, Source Analysis, Geography, English Language, English
            Literature, Essay Writing, Reading Comprehension, Computer Science and Programming.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Study and revision tools</h2>
          <p>
            Topic summaries, revision notes, worked examples, practice questions with full answers,
            printable worksheets, flashcards, self-marking quizzes, homework help, study planners,
            progress tracking, revision timetables, note-taking guides and exam technique advice.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Exam preparation</h2>
          <p>
            Past-paper style questions grouped by topic and difficulty, mark scheme guidance, common
            mistakes to avoid, timing strategy, end-of-topic tests, mock exam practice, midterm and
            final exam revision, and college entrance exam preparation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Student levels</h2>
          <p>
            Elementary school, middle school, high school, K-12 education, college and university
            foundation courses, homeschool learners, adult education and continuing professional
            development.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How students learn here</h2>
          <p>
            Self-paced study, interactive practice, spaced repetition, active recall, worked-example
            learning, problem solving, critical thinking and STEM education. Each topic starts with a
            short explanation, moves to a worked example, and finishes with questions that check
            whether the idea has actually stuck.
          </p>
        </section>
      </main>
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
